\caption{32B cross-model divergence. R1-32B provides early warning with narrow coverage; QwQ-32B provides robust significance with poor lead time. Pos.\ margin: HS balanced accuracy minus position-only baseline.}
\label{tab:32b_divergence}
\end{table}

R1-32B detects commitment 11--13 steps early with a +14.2pp margin over the position baseline, which confirms genuine representational signal, but covers only 25\% of traces at the single significant window ($W{=}1$). QwQ-32B achieves statistical significance at all window sizes with 74\% coverage, but HS probes fire later than text probes (negative lead time). The position baseline achieves higher balanced accuracy than HS probes (0.832 vs.\ 0.796) because harmful steps tend to occur later in traces; however, HS probes achieve higher precision (+4.5pp: 0.564 vs.\ 0.519) and lower FPR ($-$3.0pp: 0.217 vs.\ 0.247). A position-residualized probe (regressing out $t/T$ from hidden states before classification) reduces QwQ-32B balanced accuracy by 18.3pp (from 0.796 to 0.612), indicating that the HS advantage on this model is largely mediated by temporal position encoding. The remaining precision and FPR margins likely reflect finer-grained position encoding rather than position-independent safety structure. The divergence implies that per-model validation is essential: no single detection configuration generalizes even within the same architecture at the same scale, and a system validated on one 32B model would fail on the other.
